Large Language Model (LLM)-based program repair generates candidate patches and selects the best via a verifier. Three execution-free alternatives to costly execution-based verification have emerged, namely per-test binary classification (\cls{}), execution output generation (\cwm{}), and trajectory-level scoring (\traj{}), yet no prior work compares them under controlled conditions. We present the first systematic comparison across two code LLM families, two complexity levels, and multiple seeds under ${\leq}$8B Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) fine-tuning. At function level, \cls{} and \cwm{} are statistically equivalent (Two One-Sided Tests [TOST] $p{=}0.007$, 5 seeds): classification offers no inherent advantage over generation when outputs are short. At repository level, however, this equivalence breaks down: \cwm{} accuracy drops 18--26 percentage points below \cls{} as format collapse emerges with longer, structured outputs. Preliminary 14B results narrow all gaps to ${\sim}$1 percentage point, suggesting the divergence is capacity-contingent rather than formulation-inherent. Error complementarity across formulations motivates complexity-aware routing as a key open direction.
\end{abstract}

tool_args.new_string:
\begin{abstract}
Execution-free verification promises to replace costly test execution in Large Language Model (LLM)-based program repair, yet practitioners must choose among three fundamentally different formulations with no controlled comparison to guide the decision: per-test classification (\cls{}), execution output generation (\cwm{}), and trajectory-level scoring (\traj{}). We present the first systematic comparison, controlling for model capacity, training data, and evaluation protocol across two code LLM families (Qwen3, DeepSeek-Coder), two complexity levels, and multiple random seeds under ${\leq}$8B Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) fine-tuning. Our central finding is that \emph{code complexity moderates the classification--generation tradeoff}. When outputs are short (function-level verification), all three formulations achieve statistically equivalent accuracy. When outputs grow longer and more structured (repository-level verification), this equivalence breaks down: generation-based verification suffers format collapse, falling well below classification. Preliminary results at 14B suggest this divergence narrows with scale, pointing to a capacity-dependent rather than formulation-inherent limitation. A failure mode taxonomy and error complementarity analysis provide actionable guidance for formulation selection and motivate complexity-aware routing as a key open direction.
\end{abstract}

